    <!-- Guide Content -->
    <section class="section">
      <div class="container">
        <article class="legal-prose" style="max-width: 860px; margin: 0 auto;">
          <div class="advisory-notice" style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 32px;">
            <div class="advisory-notice-icon">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
            </div>
            <div class="advisory-notice-content">
              <h4>Immediate Right to Use &trade;</h4>
              <p>The moment Form TM-A is submitted on the official IP India portal and the government filing receipt is issued, you gain the lawful right to append the &trade; symbol to your brand name and marketing assets.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2>1. Overview of the Statutory Sequence</h2>
          <p>Indian trademark registration is managed by the Controller General of Patents, Designs and Trade Marks (CGPDTM). For applicants based in Kerala, the appropriate jurisdictional branch is the Trade Marks Registry in Chennai.</p>

          <div class="bn-table-wrap">
            <table class="bn-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Phase</th>
                  <th>Statutory Stage</th>
                  <th>Key Milestone / Document</th>
                  <th>Estimated Interval</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Step 1</strong></td>
                  <td>E-Filing on IP India</td>
                  <td>Form TM-A filed; Application Number issued</td>
                  <td>Day 1</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Step 2</strong></td>
                  <td>Formalities Check</td>
                  <td>Verification of applicant details, power of attorney, and Vienna code</td>
                  <td>1 to 2 Weeks</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Step 3</strong></td>
                  <td>Substantive Examination</td>
                  <td>Section 9 & 11 review; Examination Report generated</td>
                  <td>1 to 6 Months</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Step 4</strong></td>
                  <td>Examination Response</td>
                  <td>Formal legal reply submitted if objections are cited</td>
                  <td>Within 30 Days</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Step 5</strong></td>
                  <td>Trade Marks Journal</td>
                  <td>Mark published in weekly journal for public opposition</td>
                  <td>4 Months Window</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Step 6</strong></td>
                  <td>Registration Certificate</td>
                  <td>Official Certificate of Registration issued (&reg; symbol)</td>
                  <td>~7 to 12 Months</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h2>2. Primary Statutory Forms Under Trade Marks Rules</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Form TM-A:</strong> The foundational application form used to register any wordmark, logo, or collective mark.</li>
            <li><strong>Form TM-48:</strong> The statutory Power of Attorney authorizing an advocate or trademark agent to act on behalf of the applicant.</li>
            <li><strong>Form TM-M:</strong> Used for procedural corrections, extension requests, or filing responses to examination reports.</li>
            <li><strong>Form TM-O:</strong> Formal notice of opposition filed by a third party during the 4-month Journal window.</li>
            <li><strong>Form TM-R:</strong> Application for renewal of a registered trademark (filed every 10 years).</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>3. What Happens During Registry Examination?</h2>
          <p>The Registry Examiner analyzes whether the application complies with the Trade Marks Act, 1999:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Accepted:</strong> If the mark is distinctive and lacks conflicting prior marks, it is accepted and directly forwarded for Journal publication.</li>
            <li><strong>Objected:</strong> The Examiner issues an Examination Report citing objections under Section 9 (lack of distinctiveness/descriptiveness) or Section 11 (conflict with existing marks). The applicant must file a formal written response within 30 days.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

